    desc: "Black metal developed a visual culture as extreme as its sound. Roots run through first-wave shock rock and bands like Venom and Bathory, but the codified look came with Norway's second wave (Mayhem, Darkthrone, Emperor): corpse paint — black-and-white face paint traced to theatrical precursors like Alice Cooper and Kiss — spiked gauntlets, and logos lettered into near-illegible tangles of branches and thorns. Album covers favoured grainy monochrome photographs, snowbound forests, crypts and lake piers, often reused from older sources, while demo culture ran on xeroxed J-cards and letterzines. The imagery deliberately courts misanthropy, anti-modernity and Nordic winter. Scenes worldwide localised the grammar (France, Greece, USBM), and the look now reads through documentary photography, festival stagecraft and endless meme-era parody.",
