    desc: "The New Wave of British Heavy Metal (NWOBHM) — Iron Maiden, Saxon, Diamond Head, Motörhead at the heavier edge — built a recognisable cover-art world on small independent labels like Bronze, Neat and Ebony. Its vocabulary: airbrushed chrome and stage lighting, motorcycles, skeletal ghouls, devils and battlefield hordes, often painted fast for tight budgets. Iron Maiden's mascot Eddie, designed by Derek Riggs from 1980, became the movement's most durable image, mutating across sleeves and T-shirts. The style fused 1950s EC horror-comic ghoulishness, science-fiction paperback painting and biker-magazine bravado, and it fed directly into American power and speed metal artwork, dungeon-metal illustration and the sword-and-skull iconography still used across heavy metal today.",
