    desc: "Teddy Boys — 'teds' — emerged in working-class London around 1950 when Savile Row tailors revived Edwardian dress and young men bought into it. The uniform: long drape jackets with velvet collars, drainpipe trousers, high-necked shirts and bootlace ties, thick-soled brothel creeper shoes, and heavily greased quiffs. They took up rock'n'roll from 1954-55, becoming the template for every later British youth tribe. The history is double-edged: the same scene's fringes were involved in the 1958 Notting Hill race riots, and the style has at times been claimed by far-right revivals, which historians of the subculture note without it defining the whole. Chris Steele-Perkins' photobook The Teds (1979) fixed the revival-era image; drape tailoring and creepers remain collectable and still surface in modern rockabilly and pub-rock scenes.",
