    desc: "Traditional skinheads emerged in Britain in 1969, evolving from hard mods: cropped hair, Dr. Martens boots, Levi's jeans, braces, checked Ben Sherman shirts, MA-1 flight jackets and donkey jackets. Crucially, the first-wave scene was multiracial and musically devoted to Jamaican music — skinhead reggae via Trojan Records artists like Symarip and Desmond Dekker — with rude boy styling a clear influence. From the mid-1970s the look was appropriated by far-right gangs, a poisonous association the wider style never fully escaped: anti-racist skinheads organised explicitly in response, most visibly SHARP (Skinheads Against Racial Prejudice, founded 1987 in New York), and 2 Tone bands kept the original multiracial identity visible. Today 'trad skin' denotes the apolitical dress code and its reggae roots, distinct from the racist appropriation documented separately.",
