    sum: "Tripp pants, band tees and rubber bracelets: suburban teens assembling goth identity from the mall.",
    desc: "Mall goth describes the teenage goth vernacular of late-1990s and 2000s North America, assembled not through obscure clubs but through mall retailers — above all Hot Topic, plus Spencer's — and carried across MySpace profiles. The uniform: Tripp NYC and Lip Service wide-leg pants with straps and chains, Slipknot, Marilyn Manson and Nine Inch Nails tees, black-and-white striped armwarmers, rubber bracelets stacked to the elbow, skull-and-cross motifs, heavy eyeliner and unnaturally black dyed hair. Older goth scenes often gatekept it as fashion without substance, but it functioned as a genuine entry point and identity for a generation; the Consumer Aesthetics Research Institute documents it as a coherent aesthetic. The phrase has been revived in the 2020s as a nostalgic label for that era's look, sold back through the same channels.",
