    sum: "Teased neon-and-black hair, bows and band tees: the MySpace scene-kid look built for the webcam.",
    desc: "Scene grew out of emo's MySpace orbit but turned up the volume: hair teased into enormous backcombed volumes with extensions, clips and bows; neon-on-black colour clashes; heavy eyeliner; skinny jeans, band tees and hoodies; jewellery piled on. It was a camera-first subculture — profile pictures, comment wars and top-friends politics were the social fabric — and its stars, like Kiki Kannibal, Audree Kitching and Hannie Dropkick, became influencers before the word existed, some selling their own merchandise lines. The music mapped to crunkcore and neon-tinted pop acts; photographers in the live-music circuit canonised the look. Scene was widely mocked as it faded in the early 2010s, then rediscovered with nostalgia on TikTok, where its styling language returned as deliberate camp.",
