    sum: "Baggy pants, jerseys, masks and dreadlocks: the streetwear-metal hybrid look of Korn, Slipknot and their era.",
    desc: "Nu-metal merged metal with hip-hop sportswear and skate culture, and its visual culture followed: enormous JNCO and Kikwear jeans, sports jerseys and Adidas tracksuits (Korn famously wore them), dreadlock extensions, gauges, wristbands and pained goatee-beards. Stage identity pushed theatrical: Slipknot's matching coveralls and self-designed masks turned the band into a horror troupe, while Fred Durst's backwards red cap made a meme of mall-metal casual. Album and merchandise art — Todd McFarlane's work for Korn's Follow the Leader among it — favoured grungy textures, tribal marks, broken type and disquieting mascots rather than NWOBHM's chrome fantasy. The style crashed out of fashion in the mid-2000s, then returned strongly in the 2020s as Y2K nostalgia, with the baggy silhouette again on runways and festival stages.",
    col: [{ h: "#16161a", n: "jersey black" }, { h: "#b32025", n: "tracksuit red" }, { h: "#d8d8dc", n: "baggy grey" }, { h: "#6b4a2f", n: "dread brown" }, { h: "#2b3a4a", n: "ink navy" }],
