    p: "c. 2013–2016; lasting influence", era: "post-ironic fashion media era",
    sum: "Deliberate blandness as a style: unbranded basics, mom jeans and sensible sneakers worn as a knowing reaction to logo fashion.",
    desc: "Normcore was coined by New York trend agency K-HOLE in its 2013 report 'Youth Mode', describing adaptation through flexible sameness rather than distinctiveness, and mainstreamed by New York magazine's February 2014 coverage. What fashion media then re-described as 'dressing deliberately normal' became a full look: plain tees, mom and straight jeans, light-wash denim, zip fleeces, trainers like New Balance 990s, and zero visible branding — famously worn by figures like Jerry Seinfeld and echoed in tech-world dress codes. The irony was structural: clothing chosen to avoid signalling became a recognisable signal itself. Normcore's lineage runs through 1990s unisex basics and forward into gorpcore, dadcore and quiet-luxury stealth wealth, and the Consumer Aesthetics Research Institute documents it as a foundational 2010s aesthetic of anti-assertion.",
    col: [{ h: "#e8e6e1", n: "oat white" }, { h: "#9aa3ad", n: "heather grey" }, { h: "#3f4a5c", n: "washed navy" }, { h: "#c9b89a", n: "khaki" }, { h: "#ffffff", n: "sneaker white" }],
