    desc: "The mob wife aesthetic erupted on TikTok in January 2024 as a deliberate rebuttal to quiet luxury's bare minimalism: maximal faux and vintage fur, leopard print, leather, gold hoop jewellery, slicked dark hair and statement lips. Its costume sources are screen matriarchs — Sharon Stone's Ginger McKenna in Casino (1995), Edie Falco's Carmela Soprano in The Sopranos — filtered through decades of mafia-cinema wardrobe. Fashion press from Vogue to The Guardian documented the trend cycle in real time, including its ethical wrinkle: a rush toward vintage fur framed as acceptable precisely because secondhand. Retail moved quickly with faux-fur leopard coats and gold jewellery; the trend burned through its winter cycle, but it anchored a broader return of cinched, loud, darkly glamorous dressing and reframed vintage fur as the acceptable route back to maximalism.",
