    n: "Raygun Gothic", a: ["atomic-age pulp futurism", "retro rocket sci-fi style"],
    cat: "Technology & Retrofuturism", c: "pulp-era retrofuturist design language", e: "historical",
    o: "imagery of 1930s–50s pulp science fiction; term coined by William Gibson in 1981", g: "US pulp culture; worldwide influence",
    p: "c. 1938–1960s (the imagery); named in the 1980s", era: "pulp magazine and atomic age era",
    sum: "Curvy chrome rockets, bubble domes and ray guns: the optimistic space future imagined by golden-age pulp science fiction.",
    desc: "Raygun Gothic names the streamlined space-age imagery of Gernsback-era science fiction — William Gibson coined the term in his 1981 story The Gernsback Continuum to describe it. The vocabulary: teardrop rockets with tail fins, bubble helmets, skyroads, chrome robots, ray guns, and utopian white cities, drawn by artists like Frank R. Paul for Amazing Stories and industrialized by streamline designers such as Raymond Loewy and Norman Bel Geddes, whose futurist essays and worlds'-fair visions gave the look engineering credibility. The style spilled into toys, comic strips, film serials and cartoons (The Jetsons, 1962), then became a deliberate retro reference for later science fiction, comics and games, most influentially Fallout's retro-atomic America. It remains the anchor image of 'the future as imagined in 1950' — optimistic, aerodynamic and chrome-bright.",
    col: [{ h: "#c8ccd0", n: "polished chrome" }, { h: "#3a6ea5", n: "porthole blue" }, { h: "#e0b13c", n: "fin gold" }, { h: "#8c1f28", n: "rocket red" }, { h: "#0e1116", n: "space black" }],
