    sum: "Body-horror biotech futures: flesh machines, wet labs and engineered organisms as cyberpunk's organic dark mirror.",
    desc: "Biopunk takes cyberpunk's corporate-dystopia frame and swaps silicon for wet biology: genetic engineering, engineered plagues, vat-grown organisms and body modification under amoral corporations. The term circulated in early-1990s genre criticism (Paul Di Filippo's Ribofunk, 1996, wears it on the cover), with precursors from Greg Bear's Blood Music (1985) onward, and a screen lineage running through David Cronenberg's body horror — The Fly (1986), eXistenZ (1999) — to Jeff VanderMeer's Annihilation (2014) and its bloom of refracted biology. Visually the aesthetic means membranes, spore clouds, surgical steel, amber specimen jars and coral-like growths over clean lab light: the future as organism rather than machine. Games and concept-art communities (Bloodborne's blood-minister horror among them) keep the vocabulary productive, usually at one remove from cyberpunk's neon.",
