    p: "c. 1950s–present; peak visual identity in the 1980s", era: "atomic anxiety to oil-crisis cinema",
    sum: "Scavenged armour, rusted vehicles and desert settlements: the visual grammar of civilization's aftermath.",
    desc: "The wasteland aesthetic grew from nuclear-age fiction — On the Beach (1957) set the genre's dread — but its look was codified by George Miller's Mad Max films, above all The Road Warrior (1981): road-leather armour, mohawks, shoulder pads, spiked and supercharged vehicles, gasoline as treasure, tribal gangs across Australian desert. The formula spread through The Terminator's future flashbacks, Judge Dredd's Cursed Earth, countless Italian derivatives, and later games (Fallout's retro-atomic ruin, Rage) and festivals like Wasteland Weekend (since 2010), where fans build full costume-and-vehicle camps. The palette is rust, dust and scavenged military surplus; the grammar is salvage — every object repaired, re-purposed, re-fought over. It remains the default image of 'the world after', flexible enough to carry ecological, nuclear or plague anxieties in turn.",
    col: [{ h: "#b0873f", n: "rust ochre" }, { h: "#8a5a33", n: "oxidized brown" }, { h: "#d9c49a", n: "dust cream" }, { h: "#4a3a2f", n: "scorched umber" }, { h: "#6f7570", n: "salvage grey" }],
