    sum: "Muscular barbarians, decaying ruins and dark sorcery: the gritty, episodic branch of fantasy Robert E. Howard founded.",
    desc: "Sword and sorcery is the pulpy, personal-stakes branch of fantasy named by Fritz Leiber in 1961 and founded in Robert E. Howard's Conan stories (Weird Tales, from 1932's 'The Phoenix on the Sword'): lone adventurers, decaying pre-modern civilizations, thieving, fighting and dark magic, rather than epic quests to save worlds. Its visual identity was fixed by cover artists — above all Frank Frazetta, whose 1960s-70s Conan paintings defined the oiled-muscle, brooding-ruin, serpent-cult look, with Roy Krenkel and Boris Vallejo alongside — and carried into John Milius's Conan the Barbarian (1982) and heavy-metal album art. The mode's contrast with Tolkien-style high fantasy is structural: episodic, morally grey, sensual and violent. Grimdark fantasy descends from its grittier end, while its decadent-ruin imagery remains the default look of barbarian fantasy everywhere.",
